**Read:** [Equations, Tables, and Charts for Compressible Flow — NACA 1135](../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/sources/Compressible-Flow-NACA-1135.lingua#r_5V6PTWHXNJS515S6PMX7BN2ZHM), compressible duct relations; [Oxygen Pipeline and Piping Systems — EIGA Doc 13/20](../../../materials/chemical-feedstocks-and-supply-systems/sources/Oxygen-Pipeline-and-Piping-Systems-EIGA.lingua#r_7BBE969N5JQJES1KG9AKNBEZBV), complete piping scope for an oxygen case; revisit [Viscous gas flow and pressure-dependent conductance](../transport/Viscous-gas-flow-and-pressure-dependent-conductance.lingua#r_7MT7K55E3BZFY7JE54FZVV32Y9).

Gas density changes along a line. A liquid-style pressure-drop calculation using an arbitrary constant density may conceal a large error. First identify the regime: laminar or turbulent, continuum or rarefied, approximately isothermal or appreciably heated/cooled, steady or transient.

As a restricted example, take steady, low-Mach, isothermal ideal-gas flow through a uniform circular pipe with negligible acceleration and elevation. Darcy friction gives −dp/dx=fD ρu²/(2D). With constant mass flux G=ρu and ρ=p/(RsT), integration for constant fD gives p₁²−p₂²=fD(L/D)G²RsT. The friction factor here is Darcy, not Fanning. This approximation fails as acceleration and choking become important.

For changing cross sections or components, solve the connected balances with local states and compatible loss relations. Piping heat exchange also affects density and delivered temperature. A common header can couple users whose demands were treated separately in a steady sizing estimate.

**Work:** Derive the squared-pressure expression and recover the small-pressure-drop limit. Compare two demand schedules with the same daily amount but different peaks; explain why their required line performance differs.

